{"id":"math/nan","module":"math","qualname":"nan","name":"nan","kind":"data","parent":null,"signatures":[],"doc":"A floating-point “not a number” (NaN) value. Equivalent to the output of float('nan'). Due to the requirements of the IEEE-754 standard, math.nan and float('nan') are not considered to equal to any other numeric value, including themselves. To check whether a number is a NaN, use the isnan() function to test for NaNs instead of is or ==. Example:","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/math.html#math.nan","added":"3.5","changed":[{"version":"3.11","text":"It is now always available."}],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":null,"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"math/nextafter","module":"math","qualname":"nextafter","name":"nextafter","kind":"function","parent":null,"signatures":["nextafter(x, y, steps=1)"],"doc":"Return the floating-point value the given number of steps after x towards y.\n\nIf steps is not specified or is None, it defaults to 1.\n\nRaises a TypeError, if x or y is not a double, or if steps is not\nan integer.  Raises ValueError if steps is negative.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/math.html#math.nextafter","added":"3.9","changed":[{"version":"3.12","text":"Added the steps argument."}],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":null,"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
{"id":"math/perm","module":"math","qualname":"perm","name":"perm","kind":"function","parent":null,"signatures":["perm(n, k=None, /)"],"doc":"Number of ways to choose k items from n items without repetition and with order.\n\nEvaluates to n! / (n - k)! when k <= n and evaluates\nto zero when k > n.\n\nIf k is not specified or is None, then k defaults to n\nand the function returns n!.\n\nRaises ValueError if either of the arguments are negative.","docsUrl":"https://docs.python.org/3.15/library/math.html#math.perm","added":null,"changed":[],"deprecated":null,"availability":[],"source":null,"implDetail":false,"isAsync":false},
